  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday night through Wednesday: Weather will be warm and dry \nthrough Wednesday as subtle ridging continues over the Pacific \nNorthwest. Temperatures on Wednesday will be similar to Tuesday, but \nwith lighter winds as the north-south pressure gradient decreases.\n\nThursday through Monday: Changes in the weather pattern arrive \nThursday as the closed low over California begins to eject \nnortheastward and a frontal system with a trailing shortwave \napproaches from the west. While the frontal system looks mostly dry, \nmoisture and precipitation associated with the upper-level low \nmoving into far eastern Washington and north Idaho will bring a 30-\n70% chance of rain across far eastern Washington. The highest \nchances for Washington are across southeast Washington, including \nthe Palouse, the L-C Valley, and the Blue mountains. Across north \nIdaho, there is a 60-80% chance for rain, with the highest \nconfidence across Shoshone County and the southern Panhandle. \nAdditionally, winds will be breezy to gusty from the northwest to \nwest with the cold front. With the KPDX-KGPI increasing to +12-17 mb \nFriday morning,  there is high confidence for wind gusts greater \nthan 30 mph across central Washington and southeast Washington (60-\n90% chance). Chances for gusts greater than 30 mph have decreased \nacross the Spokane area, with now only a 10-20% chance. Blowing dust \nis not anticipated to be a significant hazard given the lack of dust \ngeneration during recent comparable wind events.  \n\nBy the weekend, confidence in the weather pattern significantly \ndecreases regarding the strength and track of the next approaching \nweather system. Ensemble analysis continues to reveal considerable \nvariance. Ensemble membership has significantly decreased on the \nidea of an upper-level low influencing the Inland Northwest, with \nonly 20% showing this scenario now. 37% of ensembles suggest the \nupper-level low to remain offshore, leaving the Inland Northwest \nunder subtle ridging and most of the precipitation west of the \nCascades. The last 43% do not have this low near the PNW at all and \nkeep the entire region under some form of ridging.  So, 80% of \nglobal ensembles now favor a drier and warmer weekend for the Inland \nNorthwest. The NBM still maintains a 30-40% chance of rain over \ncentral Washington to northeast Washington and a 10-15% chance \nacross southeast Washington and the central and southern Idaho \nPanhandle. This uncertainty is further increased by a 9-12 degreeF \nspread in the the 25th-75th percentile temperature solutions in the \nNBM across the Inland Northwest. AI ensembles still provide little \nconsensus, as the GEFS AI ensemble favors the cooler and wetter \nsolution (which is the opposite of the physics-based models) while \nthe ECMWF AIFS ensemble indicates only subtle mean troughing, \nhighlighting the uncertainty for the weekend. /vmt",
